    descriptionAdvanced: {
      de: "Als Hauptort der Krobo-Volksgruppe in der Eastern Region, ist Somanya berühmt für seine farbenfrohen Glasperlen, die hier seit Generationen in traditionellen Brennöfen hergestellt werden. Das jährliche Ngmayem-Festival feiert den Anbau von Hirse und dient als großes Treffen der Krobo-Familien aus dem ganzen Land. Darüber hinaus beherbergt die Stadt den Campus der neuen University of Environment and Sustainable Development, was einen Wandel hin zur Akademisierung markiert. Kultur K6 — traditionelles Handwerk.",
      hu: "Somanya a Keleti Régió hagyományos krobo népességű települése, amelyet az Akwapim-hegység lábainál alapítottak. A város leginkább egyedi, égetett agyag- és üveggyöngy készítő mestereiről, valamint festői környékéről, a krobo törzsi fesztiválokról ismert. Békés kisvárosi hangulata népszerű célponttá teszi a fővárosiak számára. Kultúra K6 — hagyományos kézművesség.",
      ro: "Somanya, situat la poalele dramatice ale lanțului montan Akwapim, este un centru agricol și cultural vital pentru poporul Krobo. Localitatea este recunoscută pe plan mondial pentru artizanii săi care produc mărgele din sticlă reciclată, o tehnică transmisă din generație în generație, ale cărei culori vibrante reprezintă mesaje culturale precise. Din punct de vedere agricol, regiunea este un furnizor uriaș de mango, un produs sărbătorit intens în piețele sale săptămânale. Cultură K6 — meșteșuguri tradiționale.",
      en: "Somanya is an important commercial and traditional town in the Eastern Region, functioning as the capital of the Yilo Krobo Municipal District. It is one of the principal settlements of the Krobo people and is deeply known for its vibrant indigenous culture. Somanya is nationally famous for the production of highly intricate glass beads, an ancient craft that remains a vital part of the local economy and tourism. The town is also situated at the foothills of the Akwapim-Togo ranges, providing fertile grounds for massive mango cultivation, which supplies juice processing plants nationwide. Geography K7 — indigenous crafts and agriculture."
    },
